Sotto — in-browser tutor, slice 5: turn-1 silence + tool round trip
Run: 2026-09-05, macOS 15 / M-series, Chromium via Playwright, headless,
     --enable-unsafe-webgpu --use-angle=metal. llama-server running
     throughout (task brief's realistic-machine condition was not required
     here since the whole point of this lane is the browser path, which
     never talks to :8790 — health is deliberately blocked, see the e2e
     script's own header).

SCOPE. slice-4's open issues, in the order investigated:
  (1) learner caption "cigarrera"/"cigarrara" vs the spoken "cigarra"
  (2) turn 1 sometimes produces no visible tutor caption
  (3) the second learner utterance was never heard
  (4) therefore no tool round trip

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUG 1 — VAD frame-size mismatch (root cause of (3), and of most of (2))
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Instrumented handleFrame() with per-frame RMS + a periodic
[count, maxRms, turnMode, vadSpeaking] report, plus a debug_vad metric on
every EnergyVad speech_start/speech_end. First worker instance's first
utterance was always detected correctly (speech_start -> speech_end matching
the ~2.7s clip); every later loop repeat of the SAME fake-mic wav — same
bytes, same content, comfortably above-threshold peak RMS the whole time
(observed 0.15-0.24 against a 0.02 threshold) — never fired speech_start
again, for the rest of that worker's life.

Traced to packages/voice/src/transports/web-audio.ts's AudioWorkletProcessor:
it posts one message per audio render quantum, ~2.7ms of PCM16 at 16kHz
(confirmed via the frame-count telemetry: ~752 chunks per 2000ms window).
packages/voice/src/browser-cascade/vad.ts's EnergyVad — and its own unit
tests, and the server's equivalent apps/server/src/voice/vad.ts — were all
written and verified assuming ~20ms frames (test file hard-codes
FRAME_SAMPLES = SAMPLE_RATE/50). Feeding it 2.7ms micro-frames makes the
hard "any below-threshold frame zeroes the accumulator" logic dramatically
more fragile: ordinary voiced speech has brief near-zero-crossing dips
several times a second, and at 2.7ms granularity those dips reset `aboveMs`
before it reaches minSpeechMs (300ms) almost every time. Only an unusually
loud, not-yet-gain-settled first capture happened to survive 300ms
uninterrupted; nothing after it ever did.

  Experiment: disabled autoGainControl in getUserMedia (WebAudioAdapter).
  Result: WORSE — even the first utterance stopped triggering (raw fake-mic
  level without AGC's initial boost never spiked as high). Reverted.

  Experiment: disabled noiseSuppression instead. Result: no change (still
  only the first utterance ever detected). Reverted (both back to true).

  Fix: EnergyVad.process() now internally re-chunks whatever it's given
  into fixed ~20ms evaluation windows before running the existing threshold
  logic (packages/voice/src/browser-cascade/vad.ts). SpeechBuffer, which
  still receives full-resolution frames for STT accuracy, is untouched.
  Existing unit tests (which already pass exactly-20ms frames) needed no
  changes and still pass unmodified — they exercise the re-chunker at a
  1:1 ratio.
  Verified: post-fix, speech_start/speech_end fire on every loop iteration
  of the fake-mic wav, and the second utterance is heard.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUG 2 — turn-1 LLM/STT-readiness race (root cause of (2))
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Once the VAD fix let turns complete quickly, a NEW failure surfaced: turn 1
sometimes still produced no tutor caption, with state going straight
thinking -> listening. Instrumented runTutorTurn/WebLlmEngine.chat.

worker.ts's `case 'init':` handler assigns `session = s` synchronously,
before `await`ing loadStt() then loadLlm() — so audio capture and VAD start
immediately, well before either model is ready. runTutorTurn's guard used to
read:
    if (!llmEngine) { setState('listening'); return; }
On a run where STT finished loading and transcribing FASTER than the LLM
(observed: stt_ms completing at ~53.9s while llm_load_ms didn't post until
54.3s — a ~0.4s gap, but transcribeSegment -> runTutorTurn ran inside that
window), the turn was silently dropped: no caption, no error, no retry, and
the caption for the learner's OWN utterance still displayed normally
(caption is posted before the runTutorTurn check), making the drop
invisible in the UI. This is very plausibly the same shape reported in
slice 2/3 and slice 4's own logs.

Fix (packages/voice/src/browser-cascade/worker.ts):
  - loadLlm() is now started before loadStt() is awaited (previously
    sequential: `await loadStt(); await loadLlm();`, which needlessly
    widened the race by delaying when LLM loading even begins), and its
    promise is tracked in module-level `llmLoadPromise`.
  - `runTutorTurnBody` (see BUG 3) now waits on `llmLoadPromise` (bounded to
    15s) if `llmEngine` is still null when a turn arrives, instead of
    immediately giving up. Only after that wait, if still null, does it
    degrade to the caption-only path.
Verified: `llm_wait_for_load` fired and the turn correctly proceeded once
loadLlm() resolved, instead of silently dropping.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
BUG 3 — tool-fence leaking into captions, and a WebLLM engine hang under
        barge-in (found while fixing (4))
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
With BUG 1 fixed, the second utterance's speech_start correctly fires the
server-parity barge-in this worker had never actually had:
apps/server/src/voice/session.ts's onSpeechStart() calls bargeIn(); worker.ts's
handleFrame had no equivalent call at all. Wiring `interruptSession(session)`
into the speech_start branch (matching the server) surfaced two further,
previously-unreachable bugs:

  3a. The JSON-tool fallback protocol (for Qwen3-1.7B, which rejects native
  `tools`) asks the model for a fenced ```tool ... ``` block. That block was
  only ever stripped from the FINAL aggregated text inside
  WebLlmEngine.chat's return value — too late, because llm-turn.ts's
  onTextDelta handler had already streamed the raw content out as captions
  sentence-by-sentence. Once the model actually attempted a tool call for
  the first time, the caption read literally "```tool" (or, after a first
  attempted fix, the whole fence text unstripped) instead of anything
  learner-facing.
    Fix #1: markers.ts's stripMarkers/safeReleaseIndex now treat an open
    ```tool fence the same way an open <think> block already was —
    stripped when complete, held back mid-stream when not yet closed.
    Fix #2 (needed after fix #1's own unit test caught it): the initial
    hold-back check only recognized the FULL literal "```tool" once it was
    already sitting in the buffer, so a stream that delivered "```" and
    "tool" as separate deltas released the first chunk before the second
    arrived — the same leak, differently shaped. safeReleaseIndex now also
    holds back any trailing fragment that could still grow into the 7-char
    marker. That in turn falsely flagged a block's own freshly-arrived
    CLOSING fence as a hypothetical new opening (both are "```"), splitting
    the release right before the closing fence and defeating stripMarkers's
    all-in-one-string regex again. Final fix: the ```tool check now scans
    only the portion of the buffer AFTER the last already-COMPLETE match
    (lastCompleteToolBlockEnd), so a just-closed fence is never re-examined
    as a potential new opening. Covered by a new unit test that replays the
    exact live-observed delta split ("Listo. ", "``", "`", "to",
    "ol\n{...}\n", "```", " Hecho.") and asserts the fence never reaches the
    caption text.

  3b. Barge-in itself then hung the session: `interruptSession` flips the
  AbortController's signal, but engine.interruptGenerate() (the WebLLM API
  called on abort) only sets an internal flag — it doesn't wait for the
  interrupted generation to actually unwind and release whatever
  request-serialization lock WebLLM holds. Starting a SECOND
  engine.chat.completions.create() for the next turn immediately after
  (once STT finished on the newly-captured, barge-in utterance) hung
  indefinitely: no error, no further worker output, session sat in
  `thinking` until the 90s idle timeout. Reproduced twice with identical
  symptoms before diagnosing.
    Fix: SessionState now tracks `currentTurnPromise`. runTutorTurn awaits
    any previous turn's promise (which settles quickly once genuinely
    aborted) before starting a new engine call, serializing access to the
    shared MLCEngine instead of letting two calls race it.
    This did NOT fully eliminate every timing sensitivity on its own — see
    the e2e fixture note below — but with it in place, no run since has
    hung; every run reaches the session's own 90s idle end cleanly instead.

Separately, the e2e fixture's own INTER_UTTERANCE_SILENCE_MS (2.5s) was too
tight once turn 1 could legitimately take longer (waiting out the LLM per
BUG 2's fix): the fixed-schedule fake-mic wav played utterance 2 while
turn 1 was still replying, exercising the now-correct-but-slower barge-in
path on every run instead of the intended "two sequential turns" shape.
Raised to 15s (apps/client/e2e/browser-tutor.mjs) so turn 1 finishes before
utterance 2 plays, matching how an actual learner would pace themselves.
TRAILING_SILENCE_MS was separately raised from 1.5s to 180s so the wav's own
loop point is never reached again within one session's lifetime.

Also fixed in the e2e harness: KEEP_PROFILE=1 reuses the profile's service
worker and shell cache (dist/sw.js is cache-first for any same-origin GET
outside /content/packs/**, and /tutor/tutor-worker.js falls into that
catch-all), so a rebuilt worker with new code silently kept serving the
PREVIOUS build's cached copy — several diagnostic iterations produced no
change in behavior until this was found. The script now unregisters any
service worker and drops the shell cache (not the model/content caches,
which is the entire point of KEEP_PROFILE) on the very first navigation of
each run. Also: KEEP_PROFILE=1 was preserving the PREVIOUS run's saved
vocabulary in IndexedDB, so "tool round trip: cigarra saved" passed on runs
where the save had actually failed, reading a stale leftover entry instead
of proof. seedProfile() now clears the sotto.vocabulary key every run.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
FIX #4 — tool-calling reliability (attempted, partially effective)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Even with the pipeline mechanically correct, Qwen3-1.7B (the in-browser
model; the server's much larger Qwen3.6-35B has never needed this JSON
fallback at all) is unreliable at actually calling save_vocabulary for a
"guarda la palabra X" command:
  - sometimes narrates the action in prose without ever emitting the fenced
    block ("...y la guardo" with no ```tool block at all)
  - sometimes calls the WRONG tool (show_explanation instead of
    save_vocabulary) for the same request
  - sometimes calls save_vocabulary correctly, with the correct tokenId
    resolved from the passage and the CORRECT word ("cigarra", even when
    STT itself misheard the spoken word as "sigurra" — the model recovered
    the right word from the passage context)

Strengthened withJsonToolInstruction's prompt (worker.ts) with an explicit
"do not just describe the action, emit the block" instruction and one
worked save_vocabulary example. This measurably helped (a save was observed
succeeding, with the correct word, in earlier diagnostic runs) but did not
make it reliable: the two final clean verification runs below both show the
model explaining the word again rather than saving it, on the third+ tool
attempt of the session. This is model capability, not a code defect in this
lane's plumbing — the tool call, when made, round-trips correctly end to
end (worker -> tool_call -> requestTool -> main thread's executeTool ->
respondTool -> tool_result -> continuation turn), proven live earlier in
this same diagnostic session (a save with tokenId "t6", word "cigarra",
landed in the IndexedDB vocabulary store with the correct SavedWord shape).

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHISPER MISTRANSCRIPTION (item 1) — accepted as fuzzy-match, per the brief
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
whisper-base continues to mishear both fixture phrases slightly
("cigarra" -> "cigarrera"/"cigarrara"/"cigarras"; "guarda" -> "cuarda" in
one run, correct in others) — consistent with the pre-existing, previously
documented imprecision (slice-1's own log shows the same mishearing before
any of this task's changes). Per the brief's own instruction NOT to weaken
the check to "any caption", apps/client/e2e/browser-tutor.mjs now does a
Levenshtein-distance (<=2) fuzzy match against the target word, tokenized
from the caption text with accents stripped, instead of a literal substring
check. Also reworded the fake-mic's second utterance from "Guarda la
palabra cigarra." to "Por favor, guarda la palabra cigarra." — the added
lead-in measurably fixed the "guarda"->"cuarda" confusion across every run
after that change (it recurs on "cigarra" itself, which the fuzzy match
absorbs).

